  :response-signal {:class :declined-on-merit
                    :note "IBM, the largest and most technically committed corporate buyer, walked away and built a competing OpenSim-based product (Virtual Collaboration for Lotus Sametime). IBM senior architect Peter Finn named the reason on the record: Linden Lab cut off teleportation between the main grid and IBM's private grid and shut down its OpenSim interoperability work, and 'the ability to take your Second Life avatar and teleport to your private grid with your inventory was the single most common request IBM had from its customers'. Linden Lab then discontinued Second Life Enterprise in 2010 and stopped accepting new customers. The education buyers declined on price, not merit, when the 50 percent education/non-profit land discount ended on 2011-01-01; those two declines should not be collapsed."}

  :claims
  [{:text "Michael Donnelly, worldwide head of interactive marketing at Coca-Cola, toured other brands' Second Life presences, found them deserted ('I felt like I was in The Shining'), and decided to spend anyway: 'my job is to invest in things that have never been done before'. He later conceded the decision 'had a lot to do with hype'."
    :provenance :cited :ref "R1"}
   {:text "NBA commissioner David Stern on the NBA's island: 'I think we've had 1,200 visitors. People tell us that's very, very good. But I can't say we have very precise expectations. We just want to be there.'"
    :provenance :cited :ref "R1"}
   {:text "On a random day in June 2007, Linden Lab's traffic tally scored the Sears store on IBM's Innovation Island at 281 and Coke's Virtual Thirst pavilion at 27, against 136,000 for Money Island. At least 50 major companies had entered Second Life by then; a full-time presence could cost up to $500,000 a year."
    :provenance :cited :ref "R1"}
   {:text "Second Life Enterprise customers paid around $50,000 each for the software and hardware package. Linden Lab discontinued the programme in 2010; Executive Director of Monetization Adam Nelson said it would 'honor our beta customer commitments and contracts, but will not be accepting new customers for the beta product'."
    :provenance :cited :ref "R2"}
   {:text "IBM senior architect Peter Finn stated that Linden Lab cut off teleportation between the main grid and IBM's private grid, that 'IBM was the only company ever allowed to connect to Second Life this way and when SLE was released as a standalone walled garden, support for the prototype environment IBM was using was removed', and that cross-grid teleport with inventory 'was the single most common request IBM had from its customers'. IBM had earlier launched a competing OpenSim-based product."
    :provenance :cited :ref "R2"}
   {:text "Linden Lab ended the 50 percent education and non-profit land discount effective 2011-01-01, doubling region rates for those buyers, and offered a locked-in rate only to institutions that pre-paid a 6- to 24-month renewal before 2010-12-31. A Second Life consultant with education clients described the binding constraint as the budget calendar, not the price level: 'most educational organizations have budgets that are cast in stone for the entire school year... Double their pricing in January, and there won't be sufficient funds to make it through June. And they won't have a new budget until next September.'"
    :provenance :cited :ref "R3"}
   {:text "US federal agencies obligated $346,935 to Linden Research Inc across 23 prime contract awards, from 2008 to a last award dated 2014-09-25, peaking at $114,540 in calendar 2010. Buyers included the Department of Defense, the Department of Commerce ('9 VIRTUAL WORLD ISLAND IN 2ND LIFE IN SUPPORT OF OUTREACH AND EDUCATION', $15,930, 2008-09-17), the Smithsonian Institution ($21,200, 2008-06-05) and the Department of Veterans Affairs ('VETERANS OUTREACH INITIATIVE', $3,540, 2011-10-19)."
    :provenance :cited :ref "R4"}
   {:text "No named corporate buyer published a post-mortem of its own Second Life spend. Everything above about buyer motive comes from journalists interviewing the buyers, not from the buyers' own records."
    :provenance :unverified
    :discharge "An internal review, board paper, or litigation exhibit from Coca-Cola, IBM, Sears, the NBA, or a named university, assessing its Second Life investment."}]

