 {:case-id        :gnu-emacs-fsf-demand
  :entity-type    :customer
  :for-case       :gnu-emacs-fsf
  :org            "the two successive payer classes: purchasers of FSF distribution tapes, CD-ROMs and printed manuals (1985-1990s), and FSF donors, associate members and corporate sponsors thereafter. Named parties on the record: Cygnus Support (matching donor, 1993), Lucid Inc. (a commercial dependant that supplied its own engineering rather than money), Sun Microsystems (shipped Lucid Emacs), and Red Hat, Mozilla and SUSE (2021 withdrawal)."
  :sector         "software distribution, commercial development tooling, and charitable giving"
  :period         {:from "1985" :to "2026"}

  :management-depth {:layers :unknown
                     :signer "Red Hat's 2021 funding suspension was issued as a corporate statement rather than attributed to a named officer; the FSF's donor base is individual and unpublished."
                     :provenance :unverified
                     :discharge "A named executive sponsor for any corporate FSF contribution, or a purchase approval chain for an institutional tape or manual order."}

  :failing-phase  {:phase "ACT"
                   :why "For this artifact the recipient's loop can complete without the payment step at all: GNU Emacs is redistributable under the GPL at no charge, so a user can perceive, believe, evaluate and select it and simply never reach an act of payment. The FSF's own accounts show what that does to the payer side: over 2011-2023 program-service revenue -- the part that comes from someone buying something -- never exceeded $15,039 and was $0 in four of those years, while contributions ran between $842,361 and $2,711,515."
                   :provenance :inferred}

  :problem-class  [:business-model]

  :capacity       {:scale "Free Software Foundation Inc (EIN 04-2888848) total revenue by year: 2011 $898,804; 2012 $1,310,034; 2013 $1,250,498; 2014 $1,188,318; 2015 $1,194,286; 2016 $1,294,906; 2017 $1,373,574; 2018 $2,628,996; 2019 $2,183,470; 2020 $1,149,602; 2021 $1,339,791; 2022 $1,352,816; 2023 $1,053,536. Contributions accounted for essentially all of it in every year."
                   :admits :unknown
                   :provenance :unverified
                   :discharge "A donor survey, a lapsed-donor analysis, or a corporate giving policy stating what class of free-software work the giver will and will not fund."}

  :adoption       {:what "Tape, CD-ROM and printed-manual purchases from the FSF; later associate memberships, one-off donations, and corporate matching schemes."
                   :when "1985-2026"
                   :scale "GNU's Bulletin, January 1993: 'every 150 tape orders allows FSF to hire a programmer for a year to create more free software.' Cygnus Support's 1993 matching scheme -- employees up to $1,000 each, customers and their employees at 50 percent up to $1,000 each -- was capped at $10,000 in total for the year."}

  :mess           {:symptom "The commercial party with the strongest dependency on Emacs could not get its changes accepted on its product schedule, and forked. Lucid Inc. chose Emacs as the editor and cornerstone of its C++ development environment Energize, worked with the FSF on version 19, then 'encountered unexpected technical resistance in getting their changes merged back into version 19, so they decided to release their own version of Emacs, which became Lucid Emacs 19.0'. It shipped with Energize 1.0 in April 1992."
                   :cost "A permanent fork and two parallel maintenance efforts: Lucid Emacs became XEmacs after Lucid went out of business in May 1994, and merging work between the two codebases continued for years afterwards."
                   :attributed-to "The XEmacs Internals Manual's own history section, written from the fork's side."}

  :transition-slots {:present [] :absent [] :provenance :unverified
                     :discharge "An internal document from a corporate Emacs dependant -- Lucid, Sun, or a modern equivalent -- setting out what it needed from upstream and on what terms. Public sources describe the outcome of the Lucid negotiation, not the situation either side went in with."}

  :response-signal {:class :declined-on-merit
                    :by-buyer [{:buyer "Red Hat, with Mozilla and SUSE signing the open letter" :class :declined-on-merit
                                :note "2021-03-25, 'immediately suspending all Red Hat funding of the FSF and any FSF-hosted events'. The stated ground is FSF governance, not the merit or the cost of Emacs."}
                               {:buyer "Lucid Inc., as a dependant" :class :forked-after-engagement
                                :note "Depended on Emacs for the Energize IDE, met 'unexpected technical resistance in getting their changes merged', and forked. Engaged, refused on schedule, exited by building the alternative -- neither a decline nor a purchase, and it wants a different remedy from either. The case that forced this class."}]
                    :by-buyer-classes [:declined-on-merit :forked-after-engagement]
                    :note "On 2021-03-25 Red Hat announced it was 'immediately suspending all Red Hat funding of the FSF and any FSF-hosted events' after Richard Stallman rejoined the FSF board, adding that 'many Red Hat contributors have told us they no longer plan to participate in FSF-led or backed events'; Mozilla and SUSE signed the open letter demanding the board's resignation. This is a public, dated, named decline to fund -- but the stated ground is the FSF's governance, not the merit or the cost of GNU Emacs. It is the sharpest instance in this batch of a payer walking away from an artifact it had no obligation to accept in the first place. The earlier Lucid episode is a different signal again: a dependant that engaged, was refused on schedule, and forked rather than declining or paying."}

  :claims
  [{:text "GNU's Bulletin vol. 1 no. 14 (January 1993): 'You can also order GNU software directly from the FSF -- indeed, we encourage you to do so: every 150 tape orders allows FSF to hire a programmer for a year to create more free software.'"
    :provenance :cited :ref "R1"}
   {:text "The same bulletin records the largest documented corporate contribution mechanism of the era and its ceiling: 'Cygnus Support will match gifts by its employees, and by its customers and their employees... up to a maximum of $1000 per employee, and will match donations from customers and their employees at 50% to a maximum of $1000 per customer. Cygnus Support will donate up to a total of $10,000 in 1993.'"
    :provenance :cited :ref "R1"}
   {:text "Free Software Foundation Inc (EIN 04-2888848) reported program-service revenue of $0 in 2011, 2012 (excluding $550), 2021, 2022 and 2023, and never above $15,039 in any year 2011-2023, against total revenue of $0.9m to $2.6m. The purchaser side of the FSF's model has effectively gone to zero; the donor side carries all of it."
    :provenance :cited :ref "R2"}
   {:text "Lucid Inc. chose GNU Emacs as the editor for its Energize C++ development environment, worked with the FSF toward version 19, hit 'unexpected technical resistance in getting their changes merged back into version 19', and released Lucid Emacs 19.0 with Energize 1.0 in April 1992. Lucid went out of business a few days after the 19.10 release in May 1994; Sun shipped the same code as XEmacs."
    :provenance :cited :ref "R3"}
   {:text "Red Hat, 2021-03-25: 'we are immediately suspending all Red Hat funding of the FSF and any FSF-hosted events'."
    :provenance :cited :ref "R4"}
   {:text "The FSF's revenue fell from $2,628,996 (2018) and $2,183,470 (2019) to $1,149,602 (2020). Whether that fall is attributable to the September 2019 events or to unrelated timing of large gifts is not established by the filings, which do not break contributions down by donor."
    :provenance :unverified
    :discharge "Schedule B donor detail, an FSF annual report attributing the change, or a statement from a named lapsed donor."}
   {:text "No source states how many tapes, CDs or manuals the FSF ever sold, or who bought them. The purchaser class of 1985-1995 is not publicly enumerable."
    :provenance :unverified
    :discharge "FSF order records, or pre-1998 Form 990s showing the split between sales and contributions."}]

